      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b89e1923ace0652a97d27afad7a2338ff79de024",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2186618504,
          "type": null,
          "body": "I assume a lot of the pointer cast ones involve passing possibly signed `char*` to a function with `unsigned char*` in order to do character set manipulation. That is partially just a problem in C (I don't know if `char8_t` etc. will help) but we could cast in lots of places.\n\nI looked at the format truncation ones at one point - I fixed the ones clang complained about that were always wrong. GCC can have a more expensive set of cases where it complains about possible truncation, and several of them are ones where we don't care about truncation, we're copying possibly fuzzed or buggy unknown length data and we want it to be truncated. I think gcc can be made not to warn if we do enough with the return values of `snprintf` and friends.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6764551,
            "username": "johnthacker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "John Thacker",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/99c30adacedf45514aaf711ba8a44f56322ebb53e9b550b7e3c9cd83bd6a1408?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/johnthacker"
          },
          "created_at": "2024-10-30T20:55:00.054Z",
          "updated_at": "2024-10-30T20:55:00.054Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 337174658,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 17821,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "be8e53ecca7af1ab8d75411cde438edb8c27ded7",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2186808315,
          "type": null,
          "body": "E.g., this is just plain a wrong warning:\n\n```c\nwireshark/ui/cli/tap-iostat.c:691:41: warning: ‘%u’ directive output may be truncated writing between 1 and 10 bytes into a region of size 3 [-Wformat-truncation=]\n  691 |                 snprintf(val_mag_s, 3, \"%u\", val_mag);\n      |                                         ^~\nwireshark/ui/cli/tap-iostat.c:691:40: note: directive argument in the range [5, 4294967295]\n  691 |                 snprintf(val_mag_s, 3, \"%u\", val_mag);\n      |                                        ^~~~\nwireshark/ui/cli/tap-iostat.c:691:17: note: ‘snprintf’ output between 2 and 11 bytes into a destination of size 3\n  691 |                 snprintf(val_mag_s, 3, \"%u\", val_mag);\n      |                 ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n```\n\nThe value of [val_mag](ui/cli/tap-iostat.c#L687-692) is the result of `magnitude(iot->max_vals[j], 15)`, which cannot possibly return a value larger than 15. Oddly, if you comment out the `val_mag = MAX(5, val_mag);` statement, then the warning goes away. (Changing it to `MAX(5U, val_mag)` doesn't help.) Something about performing the MAX comparison makes GCC forget that `val_mag` is already bounded above by 15.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6764551,
            "username": "johnthacker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "John Thacker",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/99c30adacedf45514aaf711ba8a44f56322ebb53e9b550b7e3c9cd83bd6a1408?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/johnthacker"
          },
          "created_at": "2024-10-31T01:29:44.982Z",
          "updated_at": "2024-10-31T01:29:55.848Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 337174658,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 17821,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "10b32fd10ae393ff777baeb259f101c7540d7db1",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2186905515,
          "type": null,
          "body": "Pointer-sign is also repeated a lot because of inlined code in header files.\n\nSomething about `ENABLE_TODO_WARNINGS` gives me horrible performance compiling some .c files, on the order of several minutes. I'm not sure what exactly.",
